1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,827
This is STAT 331, video 7. The topic is confidence intervals for beta one.

2
00:00:07,277 --> 00:00:13,741
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:14,191 --> 00:00:22,895
Type A: derive the confidence interval for beta one. Lecture 3 does it when sigma is known, and when sigma is unknown.

4
00:00:23,345 --> 00:00:33,947
Type B: compute the standard error of beta one hat and the interval, from data or from R output. Tutorial 1 and Tutorial 2 ask this.

5
00:00:34,397 --> 00:00:44,339
Type C: interpret the interval in the units of the problem, and say when it is valid. Assignment 1, question 1 c, asks about validity.

6
00:00:44,789 --> 00:01:03,626
Tutorial 1, question 1 d, asks for a 95 percent confidence interval for beta one. The estimate is minus 0.4104. To see the problem, use our own example from video 6. There, the true slope beta one is 0.5.

7
00:01:04,076 --> 00:01:11,884
We draw a sample of 10 points and fit the line. Sample 1 gives beta one hat equal to 0.600.

8
00:01:12,334 --> 00:01:15,769
Sample 2 gives 0.359.

9
00:01:16,219 --> 00:01:25,008
Sample 3 gives 0.611. Each sample gives a different estimate. None of them is exactly 0.5.

10
00:01:25,458 --> 00:01:39,026
One number does not show how far it can be from beta one. We want an interval that covers beta one with probability 0.95. This is a 95 percent confidence interval.

11
00:01:39,476 --> 00:01:51,487
The tool is the distribution of beta one hat from video 6. It is normal, with mean beta one and variance sigma squared over S x x. The green curve shows it.

12
00:01:51,937 --> 00:02:04,737
Section 2. Question type A asks you to derive the confidence interval. Lecture 3 does it in two cases: sigma known, and sigma unknown. Method card A has five steps.

13
00:02:05,187 --> 00:02:20,291
Step 1: write the pivot. A pivot contains beta one, and its distribution has no unknown parameter. With known sigma it is standard normal. With unknown sigma it is t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

14
00:02:20,741 --> 00:02:33,370
Step 2: write 1 minus alpha as the probability that the pivot is between minus q and q. The middle area is 1 minus alpha. Each tail has area alpha over 2.

15
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:48,668
Step 3: multiply all three parts by the standard deviation, or by the standard error. It is positive, so the inequalities keep their direction. The axis becomes beta one hat minus beta one.

16
00:02:49,118 --> 00:02:59,657
Step 4: get beta one alone. Multiply by minus 1, which flips the inequalities, and add beta one hat. The axis becomes beta one.

17
00:03:00,107 --> 00:03:08,277
Step 5: read the interval: beta one hat, plus or minus q times the standard deviation or the standard error.

18
00:03:08,727 --> 00:03:28,311
Card A, step 1: write the pivot. Pause the video and try it. Start from video 6, slide 24. Beta one hat is normal, with mean beta one and variance sigma squared over S x x. Its standard deviation, S D, is sigma over root S x x.

19
00:03:28,761 --> 00:03:38,212
Subtract beta one. The mean moves from beta one to 0. The variance does not change. On the right, the curve moves to 0.

20
00:03:38,662 --> 00:03:49,478
Divide by the standard deviation, sigma over root S x x. The variance becomes 1. The point beta one plus one S D moves to 1.

21
00:03:49,928 --> 00:04:02,920
Call the result Z. Z is standard normal. Z contains the unknown beta one, but its distribution has no unknown parameter. So Z is the pivot for steps 2 to 5.

22
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:23,295
Now steps 2 to 5 of card A, for known sigma. Pause the video and try it. Step 2: for a standard normal Z, the probability that Z is between minus 1.96 and 1.96 is 0.95. Each tail has area 0.025.

23
00:04:23,745 --> 00:04:30,913
Put the pivot in place of Z. Line 2 is the same event, written with beta one hat and beta one.

24
00:04:31,363 --> 00:04:46,083
Step 3: multiply all three parts by sigma over root S x x. It is positive, so the inequalities keep their direction. On the right, the axis now shows beta one hat minus beta one.

25
00:04:46,533 --> 00:05:03,024
Step 4: get beta one alone. Multiply by minus 1. This flips the inequalities. Write them in the same order again. The limits are symmetric, so they do not change. The axis is now beta one minus beta one hat.

26
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:12,839
Add beta one hat to all three parts. Now beta one is alone in the middle. On the right, the interval is centred at beta one hat.

27
00:05:13,289 --> 00:05:26,815
Step 5: read the interval. A 95 percent confidence interval for beta one is beta one hat, plus or minus 1.96 times sigma over root S x x.

28
00:05:27,265 --> 00:06:01,669
Look at the meaning with our own example from video 6. The true slope is 0.5, and sigma is 1. S x x is 82.5. So the half width is 1.96 times 1 over root 82.5, which is 1.96 times 0.1101, or 0.216. Sample 1 gave beta one hat equal to 0.600. Its interval is 0.384 to 0.816.

29
00:06:02,119 --> 00:06:12,509
Sample 2 gave 0.359. Its interval moves to the left: 0.143 to 0.575.

30
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:21,577
Sample 3 gave 0.611. Its interval is 0.395 to 0.827.

31
00:06:22,027 --> 00:06:37,899
The interval changes from sample to sample. Beta one does not change. The probability 0.95 is about the random interval. Over many samples, 95 percent of the intervals cover the true beta one.

32
00:06:38,349 --> 00:06:53,155
In practice, sigma is unknown, and Z contains sigma. So card A, step 1 needs a new pivot. Slide 31 uses three facts. Fact 1: Z, the standardized beta one hat, is standard normal.

33
00:06:53,605 --> 00:07:08,623
Fact 2: V, which is 1 over sigma squared times the sum of the squared residuals, has a chi squared distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The picture uses n minus 2 equal to 5.

34
00:07:09,073 --> 00:07:14,257
Fact 3: Z and V are independent. The slides state this without proof.

35
00:07:14,707 --> 00:07:29,385
From Lecture 1: for independent Z and V, Z over the square root of V over nu has a t distribution with nu degrees of freedom. The green t curve is like the normal curve, but its tails are heavier.

36
00:07:29,835 --> 00:07:42,421
So divide Z by the square root of V over n minus 2. The result has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The next slide simplifies this ratio.

37
00:07:42,871 --> 00:07:58,487
Write the ratio out. Pause the video and try it. The numerator is Z: beta one hat minus beta one, over sigma over root S x x. The denominator is the square root of V over n minus 2.

38
00:07:58,937 --> 00:08:12,164
The numerator has 1 over sigma. The denominator also has 1 over sigma, from the square root of 1 over sigma squared. These two factors cancel. The unknown parameter is gone.

39
00:08:12,614 --> 00:08:31,729
The denominator is now the square root of the sum of e i squared over n minus 2. That is sigma hat, from video 5. So beta one hat minus beta one, over sigma hat over root S x x, has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

40
00:08:32,179 --> 00:08:50,440
This is the same form as Z, with sigma hat in place of sigma, and t in place of the normal. The t tails are heavier, so its 95 percent cut-off is larger than 1.96. With 5 degrees of freedom, it is 2.571.

41
00:08:50,890 --> 00:09:01,407
Step 2 of card A, now with the t pivot. We need a number q. The pivot must be between minus q and q with probability 1 minus alpha.

42
00:09:01,857 --> 00:09:10,455
The green middle area is 1 minus alpha. For a 95 percent interval, 1 minus alpha is 0.95.

43
00:09:10,905 --> 00:09:27,715
Each tail has area alpha over 2. So q is the 1 minus alpha over 2 quantile of t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. We write it t, 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2. The slide finds it with software.

44
00:09:28,165 --> 00:09:39,963
The table shows q for a 95 percent interval. With 2 degrees of freedom, q is 4.303. With 5, q is 2.571.

45
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:54,343
With 10, q is 2.228. With 30, it is 2.042. With 234, it is 1.970, near the normal value 1.960.

46
00:09:54,793 --> 00:10:08,425
Small samples have heavier t tails, so they need a larger q. For 95 percent, alpha is 0.05, and q is t, 0.975, n minus 2.

47
00:10:08,875 --> 00:10:23,830
Steps 3 to 5 are the same algebra as before, with sigma hat and q. Pause the video and try it. Line 1 is step 2: the t pivot is between minus q and q with probability 0.95.

48
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:37,165
Step 3: multiply all three parts by sigma hat over root S x x. It is positive, so the inequalities stay. The axis is now beta one hat minus beta one.

49
00:10:37,615 --> 00:10:48,794
Step 4: multiply by minus 1. The inequalities flip. Write them in the same order again. The axis is now beta one minus beta one hat.

50
00:10:49,244 --> 00:10:54,108
Add beta one hat to all three parts. Beta one is alone in the middle.

51
00:10:54,558 --> 00:11:09,555
Step 5: the confidence interval for beta one is beta one hat, plus or minus t, 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2, times sigma hat over root S x x. This is slide 34.

52
00:11:10,005 --> 00:11:22,443
Slide 35 gives a short name to sigma hat over root S x x. Here is the sampling distribution of beta one hat again. Its spread is its standard deviation.

53
00:11:22,893 --> 00:11:31,277
The standard deviation of beta one hat is sigma over root S x x. It is a fixed number, but sigma is unknown.

54
00:11:31,727 --> 00:11:44,697
The standard error of beta one hat is the estimated standard deviation. Put sigma hat in place of sigma: sigma hat over root S x x. We can calculate it from the data.

55
00:11:45,147 --> 00:12:02,470
So the confidence interval is beta one hat, plus or minus t, 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2, times the standard error of beta one hat: estimate, plus or minus quantile times standard error. Type B uses this form.

56
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:15,848
Section 3. Question type B asks you to compute the standard error and the interval. The data come as a table, as in Tutorial 1, or as R output, as in Tutorial 2.

57
00:12:16,298 --> 00:12:26,581
Step 1 of method card B: get beta one hat, S x x and n. With a data table, use card B of video 4. With output, read them.

58
00:12:27,031 --> 00:12:40,172
Step 2: sigma hat is the square root of S S E over n minus 2. S S E is the sum of the squared residuals. R prints sigma hat as the residual standard error.

59
00:12:40,622 --> 00:12:49,817
Step 3: the standard error of beta one hat is sigma hat over root S x x. R prints it in the column Std. Error.

60
00:12:50,267 --> 00:13:01,061
Step 4: q is t, 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2. For 95 percent, it is t, 0.975, n minus 2.

61
00:13:01,511 --> 00:13:10,386
Step 5: the interval is beta one hat, plus or minus q times the standard error. Give the lower and the upper end point.

62
00:13:10,836 --> 00:13:35,561
A type B question from data. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 1 c: find the standard error of beta one hat. Step 1 of card B: video 4 found beta one hat, minus 0.4104, beta zero hat, 68.446, and S x x, 1294. n is 7.

63
00:13:36,011 --> 00:13:53,910
Step 2 needs the residuals. Row 1: x is 110. The fitted value is 68.4459 minus 0.410355 times 110, which is 23.307.

64
00:13:54,360 --> 00:14:05,837
The residual is y minus the fitted value: 26 minus 23.307, which is 2.693. The orange segment shows it.

65
00:14:06,287 --> 00:14:18,362
Do the same for each column, and square each residual. The sum of the squares is S S E, 104.10. Check: the residuals add to 0.

66
00:14:18,812 --> 00:14:31,761
Divide S S E by n minus 2, which is 5. Sigma hat squared is 20.820. Sigma hat is its square root, 4.5629.

67
00:14:32,211 --> 00:14:52,862
Step 3: the standard error is sigma hat over root S x x. That is 4.5629 over the square root of 1294, which is 35.972. The standard error of beta one hat is 0.1268.

68
00:14:53,312 --> 00:15:15,691
Part d. Pause the video and try it. Step 4 of card B: n minus 2 is 5. For 95 percent, q is t, 0.975, 5, which is 2.571. The green area between minus 2.571 and 2.571 is 0.95.

69
00:15:16,141 --> 00:15:27,639
Step 5: the margin is q times the standard error: 2.571 times 0.1268, which is 0.326.

70
00:15:28,089 --> 00:15:42,148
The interval is minus 0.4104 plus or minus 0.326. It goes from minus 0.736 to minus 0.084.

71
00:15:42,598 --> 00:15:53,969
On the number line, 0 is outside the interval. Every value in it is negative. So the data support a negative slope. Video 8 makes this a formal test.

72
00:15:54,419 --> 00:16:18,781
A type B question from R output. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 1 c, gives R output for 25 patients. Step 1 of card B: the slope estimate is minus 2.3283. S x x is 532.24. The output has 23 degrees of freedom, so n minus 2 is 23.

73
00:16:19,231 --> 00:16:24,266
Step 2: sigma hat is the residual standard error, 13.52.

74
00:16:24,716 --> 00:16:33,847
Step 3: the standard error of beta one hat is in the column Std. Error: 0.5859.

75
00:16:34,297 --> 00:16:52,387
Check it with the formula: 13.52 over the square root of 532.24, which is 23.070. The result is 0.586. It agrees with the output, to the rounding of sigma hat.

76
00:16:52,837 --> 00:16:59,984
Step 4: q is t, 0.975, 23, which is 2.069.

77
00:17:00,434 --> 00:17:09,671
Step 5: the margin is 2.069 times 0.5859, which is 1.212.

78
00:17:10,121 --> 00:17:25,225
The interval is minus 2.3283 plus or minus 1.212: from minus 3.540 to minus 1.116. This agrees with the tutorial solution.

79
00:17:25,675 --> 00:17:49,419
The example of slides 36 and 37 uses the brainhead data from lecture 2: brain weight in grams against head size in cubic centimetres, for 236 people. Step 1 of card B: beta one hat is 0.26082. S x x is about 30.6 million. n is 236.

80
00:17:49,869 --> 00:17:54,861
Step 2: from the lecture 2 fit, sigma hat is 72.35.

81
00:17:55,311 --> 00:18:11,653
Step 3: the standard error is 72.35 over the square root of S x x. The square root is 5536.0. So the standard error is 0.01307.

82
00:18:12,103 --> 00:18:23,004
Step 4: n minus 2 is 234. q is t, 0.975, 234, which is 1.970.

83
00:18:23,454 --> 00:18:34,377
Step 5: the margin is 1.970 times 0.01307, which is 0.02575.

84
00:18:34,827 --> 00:18:57,333
The interval is 0.26082 plus or minus 0.02575: from 0.23507 to 0.28657. Rounded, this is 0.235 to 0.287, the values on slide 36.

85
00:18:57,783 --> 00:19:18,925
The same card gives an interval for beta zero. Only step 3 changes: we need the standard error of beta zero hat. Step 1: beta zero hat is 335.432, x bar is 3637.86, and n is 236.

86
00:19:19,375 --> 00:19:35,780
Step 3: the variance of beta zero hat is from video 6 and Assignment 1, question 1 a. Put sigma hat in place of sigma: sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x.

87
00:19:36,230 --> 00:19:49,649
Put in the values: 72.35 times the square root of 1 over 236 plus 3637.86 squared over S x x.

88
00:19:50,099 --> 00:20:03,645
The two fractions are 0.00424 and 0.43182. The bar shows them. x bar is far from 0, so the second part is much larger.

89
00:20:04,095 --> 00:20:21,695
Their sum is 0.43606, and its square root is 0.66035. So the standard error is 72.35 times 0.66035, which is 47.78.

90
00:20:22,145 --> 00:20:32,044
Step 5: the margin is 1.970 times 47.78, which is 94.127.

91
00:20:32,494 --> 00:20:47,321
The interval is 335.432 plus or minus 94.127: from 241.305 to 429.559.

92
00:20:47,771 --> 00:21:03,067
Slide 37 shows both intervals as R prints them. The columns 2.5 percent and 97.5 percent are the lower and the upper end points. The intercept row agrees with our calculation.

93
00:21:03,517 --> 00:21:13,607
Section 4. Question type C asks you to interpret a confidence interval, and to say when it is valid. Method card C has four steps.

94
00:21:14,057 --> 00:21:28,948
Step 1: write the units of x and of y. Beta one is a change in the mean of y for one more unit of x, so its interval is in units of y. If y is in hundreds of dollars, convert the end points.

95
00:21:29,398 --> 00:21:45,035
Step 2: write the sentence. We are 95 percent confident that, for each one-unit increase in x, the mean of y changes by between the lower and the upper end point. Say increases or decreases, from the signs.

96
00:21:45,485 --> 00:21:59,587
Step 3: the 95 percent belongs to the method. Over many samples, about 95 percent of the intervals cover beta one. In the picture, one of 10 intervals, in red, does not cover it.

97
00:22:00,037 --> 00:22:09,786
Step 4: the 95 percent needs the model assumptions: a linear mean, independent errors, normal errors, and a constant variance.

98
00:22:10,236 --> 00:22:26,492
Slide 37 asks a question about the brainhead interval. Does it mean that the probability that beta one is between 0.235 and 0.287 is 95 percent? Pause the video and try it.

99
00:22:26,942 --> 00:22:38,035
After the data are in, the two end points are fixed numbers: 0.235 and 0.287. Beta one is also a fixed number.

100
00:22:38,485 --> 00:22:44,331
If beta one is inside the interval, the statement is true. Its probability is 1.

101
00:22:44,781 --> 00:23:03,597
If beta one is outside, the statement is false. Its probability is 0. So the answer of slide 38 is no. The 95 percent describes the method: before we take the sample, the random interval covers beta one with probability 0.95.

102
00:23:04,047 --> 00:23:21,113
Slide 39 shows this meaning with a picture. We draw samples again and again. For each sample, we build the interval in the same way. Here are the first 10 intervals, from our own simulation with n equal to 10. 10 of them cover beta one.

103
00:23:21,563 --> 00:23:30,651
Here are 40 intervals. They have different centres and different widths. The width changes because sigma hat changes from sample to sample.

104
00:23:31,101 --> 00:23:39,208
The red intervals miss the true beta one. Here, 4 of the 40 intervals miss it. So 36 of 40 cover it.

105
00:23:39,658 --> 00:23:55,637
With 4000 samples, 94.9 percent of the intervals cover beta one. This is near 95 percent. Any given interval may or may not cover the truth. On average, 95 percent cover it.

106
00:23:56,087 --> 00:24:09,996
Now the words for Tutorial 1, question 1 d. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: x is machine hours per month. y is the monthly maintenance cost in hundreds of dollars.

107
00:24:10,446 --> 00:24:29,155
So multiply the end points by 100 dollars. Minus 0.736 becomes minus 73.6 dollars. Minus 0.084 becomes minus 8.4 dollars. The number line shows the interval in dollars.

108
00:24:29,605 --> 00:24:47,120
Step 2: both end points are negative, so the mean cost decreases. We are 95 percent confident that, for each additional machine hour per month, the mean monthly maintenance cost is between 8.4 and 73.6 dollars lower.

109
00:24:47,570 --> 00:24:53,778
Step 3: 95 percent confident refers to the method, as on slide 39.

110
00:24:54,228 --> 00:25:13,044
A type C question. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 1 c: interpret the estimated association, and give the interval. Step 1 of card C: x is the severity index. y is the satisfaction score. A higher score means more satisfied.

111
00:25:13,494 --> 00:25:27,830
First the estimate, as in video 4. Beta one hat is minus 2.3283. So for each one-unit increase in severity, the estimated mean satisfaction is 2.33 points lower.

112
00:25:28,280 --> 00:25:50,125
Step 2: the interval from Section 3 is minus 3.540 to minus 1.116. Both ends are negative. We are 95 percent confident that, for each one-unit increase in severity, the mean satisfaction is between 1.12 and 3.54 points lower.

113
00:25:50,575 --> 00:25:59,557
Step 3: the 95 percent describes the method. About 95 percent of the intervals built this way cover beta one.

114
00:26:00,007 --> 00:26:07,452
Step 4: this holds if the four model assumptions hold. The next problem asks what happens when one fails.

115
00:26:07,902 --> 00:26:24,179
Assignment 1, question 1 c. If the independence assumption does not hold, is the usual confidence interval for beta one valid? Explain. Pause the video and try it. This is step 4 of card C: check the assumptions.

116
00:26:24,629 --> 00:26:45,024
Find each place where the derivation used independence. First, the variance. The variance of the sum of w i y i equals sigma squared times the sum of w i squared. We added the variances. That needs independence. With correlated errors, covariance terms appear.

117
00:26:45,474 --> 00:26:54,071
Second, the chi squared distribution of the sum of squared residuals over sigma squared. It needs independent normal errors.

118
00:26:54,521 --> 00:26:58,724
Third, the independence of Z and V. The t result needs it.

119
00:26:59,174 --> 00:27:05,979
So the standard error does not estimate the true standard deviation, and the t quantile is not correct.

120
00:27:06,429 --> 00:27:20,765
The answer is no. Beta one hat is still unbiased, because its mean needs only errors with mean zero. But the usual interval is not valid. Its true coverage is not 95 percent in general.

121
00:27:21,215 --> 00:27:38,559
Here is a check with our own simulation. We use 20 x values and 4000 samples, with the usual interval. With independent errors, 94.5 percent of the intervals cover beta one. This is near 95 percent.

122
00:27:39,009 --> 00:27:54,412
Now make the errors correlated. Each error is 0.6 times the previous error, plus new noise. Neighbouring points have similar errors. Now only 69.2 percent of the intervals cover beta one.

123
00:27:54,862 --> 00:28:03,182
The standard error is too small in this case, so the interval is too narrow. So the interval needs the independence assumption.

124
00:28:03,632 --> 00:28:22,576
To recap. Card A derives the interval. Write the pivot, normal when sigma is known, t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom when sigma is unknown. Put it between minus q and q, multiply by the standard deviation or the standard error, and get beta one alone.

125
00:28:23,026 --> 00:28:35,421
Card B computes it: beta one hat, S x x and n; sigma hat; the standard error; the quantile q; and the estimate plus or minus q times the standard error.

126
00:28:35,871 --> 00:28:51,743
Card C interprets it: the units, the sentence with 95 percent confident, the 95 percent as a property of the method, and the four assumptions. The next video uses the same t pivot for hypothesis tests.
